                  <provenance type="#i"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_618">Hounslow, Middlesex.
                        Trinitarian convent</orgName><p><q>Iste liber constat fratri ^radulpho
                           beckwyth^ [substituted for 'Johanni . . . . . d . .] manet in domo de
                           hondeslow, 148[.]</q> (at end).</p><p><q>Fratri Johanni Sa. .dys
                           [?Sandys] de hondeslow in Con'</q> (at beginning).</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">Thomas
                        Rawlinson</persName> (1681-1725).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">Thomas
                     Martin</persName>.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">Francis
                        Blomefield</persName> (d. 1752).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">C. Hunt</persName> of
                     Wicksworth, 1820 (sale, Sotheby's, 3 May 1853).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">E. Waterton</persName>,
                     1855.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">D. A. Cruse</persName>,
                     1896.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Anonymous sale, Sotheby's, 27 November 1945,
                     lot 357.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Francis Edwards Ltd put it up for sale at
                     £100 in January 1946. It remained in their hands, appearing in catalogue 700
                     (1950), no. 323. When the book was first recorded in the second edition of
                     Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964), the Francis Edwards sale was given
                     as the last known location.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">The book remained untraced in the supplement
                     to Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1987), but with a more recent
                     observation: J. L. Beijers auction, Utrecht, 21 April 1959, lot
                     487.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">MLGB3 (2009-2024) recorded that that the book
                     went to Gouda town library.</provenance>
               </history>
